===== loop/work/statusline.ps1 =====
   1: # statusline.ps1 - the Claude Code status bar for ai4good.
   2: #
   3: # Claude Code pipes a JSON blob on stdin describing the session. Five things earn their width
   4: # (founder, 2026-07-31): the model, the PM requirement, the dev leaf, the branch, and how much
   5: # context is left. The folder name and the dollar figure were dropped - the folder is already
   6: # obvious from the terminal, and the spend was noise.
   7: #
   8: # Attribution is DERIVED from the branch (AI4DEV-36) - the same chain the stamp prints, read
   9: # through the same work-lib helpers, never re-derived here. A status bar confidently naming the
  10: # WRONG item is worse than one naming none.
  11: #
  12: # This file was missed by the AI4DEV-36 cut-over and kept reading the deleted bindings/ directory
  13: # by hand-built path, so it reported `unattributed` for everything while spending four
  14: # subprocesses per refresh to get there. It slipped through because the cut-over searched for
  15: # CALLS to the binding functions and this file never called them - it rebuilt the path itself.
  16: # The lesson is general: grepping for an API misses code that reimplemented it.
  17: #
  18: # COST MATTERS HERE. Claude Code spawns a fresh PowerShell for every refresh, which on Windows is
  19: # already a few hundred milliseconds; each extra subprocess is paid on every keystroke-ish redraw
  20: # and shows up as a client that looks perpetually busy. So: ONE git call (rev-parse takes several
  21: # arguments at once), and no `git status --porcelain` - the dirty marker was the least valuable
  22: # of the fields and by far the most expensive.
  23: #
  24: # Never throws. A status line that errors is noise in the founder's face on every keystroke.
  25: # Set AI4GOOD_STATUSLINE_DUMP=1 to capture the raw stdin JSON for field discovery.
  26: 
  27: $ErrorActionPreference = 'SilentlyContinue'
  28: 
  29: # Write the line as UTF-8 BYTES straight to the standard output handle. PowerShell 5.1 would
  30: # otherwise re-encode Write-Output through the console code page, which shreds the bar's block
  31: # glyphs into replacement characters when Claude Code spawns this without a real console.
  32: function Write-Line([string]$text) {
  33:     try {
  34:         $out = [Console]::OpenStandardOutput()
  35:         $bytes = [System.Text.Encoding]::UTF8.GetBytes($text + "`n")
  36:         $out.Write($bytes, 0, $bytes.Length)
  37:         $out.Flush()
  38:     } catch {
  39:         Write-Output $text
  40:     }
  41: }
  42: 
  43: function Get-Field($obj, [string[]]$path) {
  44:     $cur = $obj
  45:     foreach ($p in $path) {
  46:         if ($null -eq $cur) { return $null }
  47:         $cur = $cur.$p
  48:     }
  49:     return $cur
  50: }
  51: 
  52: try {
  53:     $raw = [Console]::In.ReadToEnd()
  54:     $j = $null
  55:     if ($raw) {
  56:         if ($env:AI4GOOD_STATUSLINE_DUMP -eq '1') {
  57:             $dump = Join-Path $env:TEMP 'ai4good-statusline-input.json'
  58:             [System.IO.File]::WriteAllText($dump, $raw)
  59:         }
  60:         try { $j = ConvertFrom-Json $raw } catch { }
  61:     }
  62: 
  63:     # SENSOR. The rate-limit windows (five_hour, seven_day, and the per-model weekly ones) are
  64:     # delivered ONLY to the status line - hooks get a different payload entirely - so this is the
  65:     # single place in the system that can see them. Snapshot them where a hook can read them.
  66:     # Written on EVERY refresh including when the field is absent, so a reader can tell "no
  67:     # reading yet" from "this build does not report windows" - a missing file and a null field
  68:     # mean different things and must not look alike.
  69:     #
  70:     # IT ALSO WRITES THE VERDICT, AND IT WRITES IT FIRST. A per-tool checkpoint is paid on every
  71:     # tool call in the system, so it cannot afford to run the gauge; it reads one line and matches
  72:     # its first token. That line is composed here, where the numbers already are.
  73:     #
  74:     # THE ORDER AND THE SINGLE try ARE THE INVARIANT, not tidiness. Verdict first, snapshot
  75:     # second, one envelope: a verdict write that fails therefore also skips the snapshot write, so
  76:     # the verdict file can never be OLDER than the snapshot beside it. "A snapshot at 95% sitting
  77:     # beside a stuck OK verdict" is not defended against here - it is impossible. The residual is
  78:     # the opposite case, a verdict one refresh NEWER than its snapshot, which is conservative (the
  79:     # alarm fires on the newest data) and heals at the next refresh.
  80:     #
  81:     # THE PATH FORMULA IS THE LIBRARY'S, never rebuilt here. This file already shipped one bug of
  82:     # exactly that kind - it hand-built the path to the old bindings directory instead of calling
  83:     # the helper, and the cut-over that deleted the helper's callers could not see it.
  84:     try {
  85:         . (Join-Path $PSScriptRoot 'window-lib.ps1')
  86:         $snapDir = Get-WindowDir
  87:         if (-not (Test-Path $snapDir)) { New-Item -ItemType Directory -Force $snapDir | Out-Null }
  88:         $snap = [ordered]@{
  89:             capturedAt = (Get-Date).ToUniversalTime().ToString('o')
  90:             sessionId  = [string](Get-Field $j @('session_id'))
  91:             version    = [string](Get-Field $j @('version'))
  92:             rateLimits = (Get-Field $j @('rate_limits'))
  93:         }
  94:         $utf8 = New-Object System.Text.UTF8Encoding($false)
  95:         [System.IO.File]::WriteAllText(
  96:             (Get-WindowVerdictPath),
  97:             ((Format-WindowVerdictLine (Get-WindowVerdict -Snapshot $snap)) + "`n"),
  98:             $utf8)
  99:         [System.IO.File]::WriteAllText(
 100:             (Get-WindowSnapshotPath),
 101:             ($snap | ConvertTo-Json -Depth 8),
 102:             $utf8)
 103:     } catch { }
 104: 
 105:     $parts = @()
 106: 
 107:     # 1. model - which brain is answering. Cheap to lose track of, expensive to be wrong about.
 108:     $model = Get-Field $j @('model','display_name')
 109:     if (-not $model) { $model = Get-Field $j @('model','id') }
 110:     if ($model) { $parts += $model }
 111: 
 112:     # 2/3/4. the binding, the leaf, and the branch. All three need the repo root.
 113:     $dir = Get-Field $j @('workspace','current_dir')
 114:     if (-not $dir) { $dir = Get-Field $j @('cwd') }
 115:     if (-not $dir) { $dir = (Get-Location).Path }
 116: 
 117:     # ONE git call for both facts.
 118:     $g = @(& git -C $dir rev-parse --show-toplevel --abbrev-ref HEAD 2>$null)
 119:     if ($g.Count -ge 2) {
 120:         $root = $g[0].Trim()
 121:         $branch = $g[1].Trim()
 122:         if (-not $branch) { $branch = '?' }
 123: 
 124:         . (Join-Path $PSScriptRoot 'work-lib.ps1')
 125: 
 126:         # The chain, exactly as the stamp resolves it: branch names the item, the cache names its
 127:         # parents. Ids carry a short label (founder rule), truncated hard because a status bar has
 128:         # no room for a sentence. Cache miss shows the bare item - never an invented root.
 129:         $ids = @()
 130:         foreach ($m in [regex]::Matches($branch, '(?i)(?<![\p{L}\p{N}])AI4(DEV|PM)-0*([0-9]{1,7})(?![\p{L}\p{N}])')) {
 131:             $id = 'AI4' + $m.Groups[1].Value.ToUpperInvariant() + '-' + [string][int]$m.Groups[2].Value
 132:             if ($ids -notcontains $id) { $ids += $id }
 133:         }
 134:         $item = if ($ids.Count -eq 1) { $ids[0] } else { '' }
 135: 
 136:         $held = $null
 137:         try { $held = Get-HeldItem } catch { }
 138:         if (-not $item -and $held -and ([string]$held.branch) -eq $branch -and (Test-ItemId ([string]$held.itemId))) {
 139:             $item = [string]$held.itemId
 140:         }
 141: 
 142:         if (-not $item) {
 143:             $parts += $(if ($ids.Count -gt 1) { 'AMBIGUOUS' } else { 'no item' })
 144:         }
 145:         else {
 146:             $chain = $null
 147:             try { $chain = Get-Chain $branch } catch { }
 148:             if (Test-Chain $chain $branch $item) {
 149:                 $short = @()
 150:                 foreach ($n in @($chain.chain)) {
 151:                     $l = ([string]$n.label -replace '[\r\n|]', ' ').Trim()
 152:                     if ($l.Length -gt 16) { $l = $l.Substring(0, 15) + [string][char]0x2026 }
 153:                     $short += $(if ($l) { '{0} ({1})' -f $n.id, $l } else { [string]$n.id })
 154:                 }
 155:                 $parts += ($short -join ' > ')
 156:             }
 157:             else {
 158:                 $parts += $item
 159:             }
 160:         }
 161: 
 162:         $parts += $branch
 163:     }
 164: 
 165:     # 5. context remaining, as a bar. Emitted by codepoint so the file itself stays ASCII and
 166:     #    cannot be mangled by whatever encoding PowerShell 5.1 decides to read it with.
 167:     $pct = Get-Field $j @('context_window','used_percentage')
 168:     $full  = [string][char]0x2588
 169:     $light = [string][char]0x2591
 170:     if ($null -ne $pct) {
 171:         $used = [int][math]::Round([double]$pct)
 172:         if ($used -lt 0) { $used = 0 }
 173:         if ($used -gt 100) { $used = 100 }
 174:         $filled = [int][math]::Floor($used * 20 / 100)
 175:         $bar = ($full * $filled) + ($light * (20 - $filled))
 176:         $parts += ('[' + $bar + '] ' + $used + '%')
 177:     } else {
 178:         # Never invent a number the payload did not supply.
 179:         $parts += ('[' + ($light * 20) + '] ?%')
 180:     }
 181: 
 182:     Write-Line ($parts -join ' | ')
 183: } catch {
 184:     # Last resort: say something true rather than nothing, and never a stack trace.
 185:     Write-Line 'ai4good'
 186: }
 187: exit 0

===== loop/work/window-gauge.ps1 =====
   1: # window-gauge.ps1 - read the Anthropic usage windows and say what may proceed.
   2: #
   3: # WHERE THE NUMBERS COME FROM. Claude Code delivers `rate_limits` to the STATUS LINE and nowhere
   4: # else - hooks receive a different payload entirely - so statusline.ps1 snapshots it on every
   5: # refresh and this script reads that snapshot.
   6: #
   7: # ONE LINE ONLY (founder 2026-08-06, the number moved to 85 on 2026-08-12): OK below the pause
   8: # line, PAUSE at it, UNKNOWN when the reading cannot be trusted. Two consequences worth holding
   9: # on to:
  10: #   1. The reading is only as fresh as the last status-line refresh, which happens on every turn
  11: #      of the interactive session. While the coordinator is dormant, nothing updates it - which
  12: #      is exactly why the conductor sends a keep-alive pulse: a pulse wakes the coordinator, the
  13: #      wake refreshes the status line, and the refresh refreshes this gauge. The heartbeat is
  14: #      what keeps the gauge honest, not a timer inside this script.
  15: #   2. A stale reading is NOT a low reading. Age is reported and, past the limit, the verdict is
  16: #      UNKNOWN - never OK. Treating "I cannot see" as "all clear" is how a guard fails silently.
  17: #
  18: # WHAT IT DOES NOT DO. It reads and reports. It never pauses anything, never messages an agent,
  19: # never waits. The coordinator owns every decision; this is its instrument, not a second
  20: # authority. A gauge that acts is a brake nobody can see.
  21: #
  22: # THE VERDICT LOGIC LIVES IN window-lib.ps1. This file is the COMMAND LINE over it: the flags, the
  23: # human lines and the exit codes. The status line must compute the same verdict in its own
  24: # process (it cannot afford a second powershell spawn per refresh) and so must the spawn gate and
  25: # the prompt stamp, so the logic is a library and every reader calls it. One implementation, or
  26: # the readers drift - which is a failure this repository has already had once.
  27: #
  28: # The account reports two windows today (five_hour, seven_day). The binary supports three more
  29: # (seven_day_overage_included, seven_day_sonnet, seven_day_opus) which appear on other plans, so
  30: # every window present is evaluated by name rather than assumed - a plan change must not silently
  31: # narrow the guard to the windows we happened to know about.
  32: 
  33: [CmdletBinding()]
  34: param(
  35:     # PAUSE line - founder's number (2026-08-12): stop work at 85 percent of a window. This
  36:     # supersedes the 90 of 2026-08-06; only the number moved, the one-line principle below is
  37:     # unchanged. The same number is window-lib.ps1's own default, for the readers that call the
  38:     # library directly.
  39:     [int]$PauseAt = 85,
  40: 
  41:     # There is deliberately NO second, lower line (founder 2026-08-06). A "start nothing new" band
  42:     # would have to be justified by the cost of a sitting, which nobody has measured, so it
  43:     # could only ever be a number that felt safe. One line, and a sitting that meets it mid-flight
  44:     # parks at its last committed work item - the per-work-item commits are the recovery.
  45: 
  46:     # Older than this and the gauge admits it cannot see, rather than reporting a stale number as
  47:     # if it were current.
  48:     [int]$StaleMinutes = 15,
  49: 
  50:     # Emit the machine shape instead of the human lines.
  51:     [switch]$Json,
  52: 
  53:     # Read a different snapshot. Exists so the mechanism can be exercised end to end against
  54:     # synthetic readings without spending a single token, and without disturbing the live file
  55:     # the status line is writing. Defaults to the real one, so no caller has to know.
  56:     [string]$SnapshotPath = '',
  57: 
  58:     # Exit 0 only when work may proceed (verdict OK). For use as a wait condition: the coordinator
  59:     # parks and polls this until it succeeds, rather than sleeping on a computed duration - the
  60:     # window is authoritative about its own reset, our arithmetic is not.
  61:     [switch]$ExitOnReady
  62: )
  63: 
  64: $ErrorActionPreference = 'SilentlyContinue'
  65: 
  66: . (Join-Path $PSScriptRoot 'window-lib.ps1')
  67: 
  68: function Emit($r) {
  69:     if ($Json) {
  70:         $r | ConvertTo-Json -Depth 6
  71:     } else {
  72:         $head = "WINDOW  {0}" -f $r.verdict
  73:         if ($r.reason) { $head += "  - " + $r.reason }
  74:         Write-Output $head
  75:         foreach ($w in $r.windows) {
  76:             Write-Output ("        {0,-28} {1,3}%   resets {2}  (in {3} min)" -f $w.name, $w.percent, $w.resetsLocal, $w.resetsInMin)
  77:         }
  78:         if ($null -ne $r.readingAgeMin) {
  79:             Write-Output ("        reading is {0} min old" -f $r.readingAgeMin)
  80:         }
  81:     }
  82:     if ($ExitOnReady) { if ($r.verdict -eq 'OK') { exit 0 } else { exit 1 } }
  83:     exit 0
  84: }
  85: 
  86: Emit (Get-WindowVerdict -SnapshotPath $SnapshotPath -PauseAt $PauseAt -StaleMinutes $StaleMinutes)

===== loop/work/window-gate.ps1 =====
   1: # window-gate.ps1 - PreToolUse on the Agent tool: no NEW work starts once the window is spent.
   2: #
   3: # WHY THE SPAWN BOUNDARY. The line is 85 percent (founder 2026-08-12), and running work is never
   4: # interrupted - a sitting killed mid-write leaves a record that lies about where the work got to.
   5: # So the one place to act is the moment somebody tries to start something NEW, and in this relay
   6: # every new thing is an Agent spawn: coordinator to conductor, conductor to sitting, conductor to
   7: # reviewer-runner, orchestrator to executor and to mechanical. One hook entry covers every one of
   8: # them. Measured for this item: PreToolUse fires for Agent calls made INSIDE a spawned agent, and
   9: # the deny reason reaches the denied actor transcript.
  10: #
  11: # THIS DECIDES NOTHING. The founder set the line; the coordinator owns every judgement about work.
  12: # This is the same class of machinery as the prompt stamp: it applies a standing decision
  13: # mechanically, at the moment it bites, and it is deliberately incapable of anything else. It
  14: # cannot stop running work, it cannot message anyone, and it cannot be turned off by the actor it
  15: # denies.
  16: #
  17: # THE DENY REASON CARRIES THE CHOREOGRAPHY. The reason string reaches the model that tried to
  18: # spawn, so it says what to do: write state, commit, push, report PARKED to your spawner, end.
  19: # Putting it here rather than in five role contracts means the instruction arrives at the moment
  20: # it is needed, and cannot drift between contracts.
  21: #
  22: # IT COMPUTES, IT DOES NOT READ THE VERDICT LINE. A spawn is rare, so the full gauge logic is
  23: # affordable here, and this is the one checkpoint that must never act on a stale composition. The
  24: # per-tool alarm is the dumb reader; this is not.
  25: #
  26: # FAILURE IS OPEN, BUT NEVER MUTE. A broken guard must not stop spawns - that would halt the whole
  27: # relay over a bug in a guard. But it says so, in both channels, so a guard that has quietly
  28: # stopped guarding cannot look exactly like a clear window.
  29: #
  30: # Contract: PreToolUse, matcher Agent. Exit 0 always; the decision is the JSON on stdout.
  31: 
  32: $ErrorActionPreference = 'SilentlyContinue'
  33: 
  34: # additionalContext is the MODEL-visible field on an allow; systemMessage is shown to the USER.
  35: # Both are emitted for a warning, because the two readers can act on different things: the model
  36: # can hold off on more work, the founder can fix the sensor.
  37: function Write-Decision([string]$decision, [string]$reason, [string]$context, [string]$userMessage) {
  38:     $hso = [ordered]@{
  39:         hookEventName          = 'PreToolUse'
  40:         permissionDecision     = $decision
  41:         permissionDecisionReason = $reason
  42:     }
  43:     if ($context) { $hso['additionalContext'] = $context }
  44:     $out = [ordered]@{ hookSpecificOutput = $hso }
  45:     if ($userMessage) { $out['systemMessage'] = $userMessage }
  46:     Write-Output ($out | ConvertTo-Json -Depth 6 -Compress)
  47: }
  48: 
  49: try {
  50:     # Drain stdin so the caller pipe is never left waiting. The payload is not needed: the matcher
  51:     # already selected the Agent tool, and the verdict comes from the snapshot, not from the call.
  52:     if ([Console]::IsInputRedirected) { [void][Console]::In.ReadToEnd() }
  53: 
  54:     . (Join-Path $PSScriptRoot 'window-lib.ps1')
  55:     $v = Get-WindowVerdict
  56: 
  57:     if ($v.verdict -eq 'PAUSE') {
  58:         $reset = '?'
  59:         foreach ($w in @($v.windows)) {
  60:             if (([string]$w.name) -eq ([string]$v.worstWindow)) { $reset = [string]$w.resetsLocal; break }
  61:         }
  62:         $reason = @"
  63: WINDOW GUARD: no new work may start. $($v.worstWindow) is at $($v.worstPercent)% of its usage window (the line is $($script:WindowPauseLine)%), and it resets at $reset local. Reading: $($v.reason)
  64: 
  65: This spawn is refused, and it is the only thing refused - work already running is never interrupted.
  66: 
  67: Do this now, in this order:
  68:   1. finish or abandon the step you are on, and write your state to disk;
  69:   2. commit and push, so the work survives;
  70:   3. report PARKED - window low to whoever spawned you, naming the item, the next phase and the pushed head;
  71:   4. end.
  72: 
  73: Your spawner does the same, one level at a time, until the conductor parks the item with a park note. The coordinator restarts the work when the window reopens - one item at a time. Nobody waits inside a running agent.
  74: "@
  75:         Write-Decision 'deny' $reason '' ('WINDOW GUARD: spawn denied - {0} at {1}% (line {2}%), resets {3}.' -f $v.worstWindow, $v.worstPercent, $script:WindowPauseLine, $reset)
  76:         exit 0
  77:     }
  78: 
  79:     if ($v.verdict -eq 'UNKNOWN') {
  80:         # A broken instrument is not a spent window. UNKNOWN reports loudly and halts nothing -
  81:         # the same rule attribution follows. Never let it pass silently as OK.
  82:         $warn = ('WINDOW GUARD: the usage-window sensor is UNKNOWN and cannot be trusted - {0}. This spawn is ALLOWED: a broken instrument is not a reason to stop working. Nobody can see how much of the window is left, so keep the work small and say so in your report.' -f $v.reason)
  83:         Write-Decision 'allow' 'window sensor unreadable - allowed with a warning' $warn $warn
  84:         exit 0
  85:     }
  86: 
  87:     # OK - silent. This runs at every spawn in the relay; a line of chatter here is a line in
  88:     # every actor transcript forever.
  89:     exit 0
  90: }
  91: catch {
  92:     # Fail OPEN, and say so. A guard that breaks silently is indistinguishable from a clear window.
  93:     try {
  94:         $msg = ('WINDOW GUARD FAILED and is allowing this spawn unguarded: ' + ($_.Exception.Message -replace '[\r\n]', ' ') + '. The usage window is NOT being checked at spawn boundaries until this is fixed.')
  95:         Write-Decision 'allow' 'window guard error - failing open' $msg $msg
  96:     }
  97:     catch { }
  98:     exit 0
  99: }

===== loop/work/stamp-hook.ps1 =====
   1: # stamp-hook.ps1 - UserPromptSubmit hook: the WORKING ON stamp.
   2: #
   3: # Prints EXACTLY TWO LINES before every prompt, always, in one fixed format:
   4: #
   5: #   WORKING ON  <root> > <parents...> > <item>
   6: #   IN          wt <folder> - branch <branch>
   7: #
   8: # Line 1 is the conclusion. Line 2 is WHAT THE CONCLUSION WAS DERIVED FROM. That pairing is the
   9: # whole point: every past incident was line 1 disagreeing with line 2 while only line 1 was
  10: # visible. Anything else - a conflict, an unresolved chain, a git state - APPENDS below. The two
  11: # lines never change shape, so they are always in the same place and always mean the same thing.
  12: #
  13: # ATTRIBUTION IS DERIVED, NEVER DECLARED. cwd -> git worktree -> branch -> exactly one item id.
  14: # The held item (written by /work) is a CROSS-CHECK ONLY: it can raise CONFLICT or fill a gap the
  15: # branch left empty, but it can NEVER override the branch. A stale held item therefore makes the
  16: # stamp louder, never wronger - the inverse of the folder-keyed binding this replaces, which
  17: # silently substituted itself for reality (AI4DEV-24: a whole run stamped against the wrong item).
  18: #
  19: # NEVER CALLS LINEAR. Titles and chains come from a cache written by /work. A cache miss degrades
  20: # to the bare item id with a note - it never guesses, and it never blocks.
  21: #
  22: # NEVER SILENT. On any failure this prints "stamp error: <what>" rather than something plausible.
  23: # The dangerous failure is not a wrong stamp; it is no stamp becoming normal, after which nobody
  24: # reads the line at all.
  25: 
  26: # The session id normally arrives in the hook payload on stdin. The BANNER cannot forward stdin -
  27: # it runs the stamp in the same process AFTER draining the payload itself - so it passes the id it
  28: # parsed as this parameter instead. The parameter wins when present; the stdin read is the
  29: # fallback. Same format rule as the stdin path, enforced below.
  30: param([string]$SessionId = '')
  31: 
  32: $ErrorActionPreference = 'Stop'
  33: $script:AgentLines = @()
  34: $script:BatchAttr = ''
  35: 
  36: # EVERY LINE NAMES ITS ACTOR (founder 2026-08-07). A stamp is read across several concurrent
  37: # actors, so a line that does not say who is speaking is ambiguous exactly when it matters. The
  38: # actor is DERIVED like everything else here - the main checkout is the coordinator, a worktree
  39: # under .claude/worktrees is an agent - never declared, so it cannot drift.
  40: $script:Actor = 'SESSION'
  41: 
  42: function Emit([string]$l1, [string]$l2, [string[]]$extra, [string]$pm, [string]$dev) {
  43:     # EVERY HUMAN LINE CARRIES THE SESSION PREFIX (founder 2026-08-11: "I want session id print
  44:     # on every line of the stamp - prefix"). One formatter, applied at the single output point,
  45:     # so a forwarded agent line and a warning line cannot drift from the actor lines. The
  46:     # machine tag stays UNPREFIXED on purpose: parsers anchor on its opening bracket, and it
  47:     # already carries the session as an attribute — humans get the prefix, machines the field.
  48:     # Runs with no session id (agent child re-runs, manual invocations) print no prefix.
  49:     $pfx = ''
  50:     if ($script:SessionId -and $script:SessionId.Length -ge 8) { $pfx = ('[' + $script:SessionId.Substring(0, 8) + '] ') }
  51:     Write-Output ($pfx + $script:Actor.PadRight(12) + 'WORKING ON  ' + $l1)
  52:     Write-Output ($pfx + $script:Actor.PadRight(12) + 'IN          ' + $l2)
  53:     # The batch attribute is ADDITIVE (founder 2026-08-11): a batched partner must reach the
  54:     # machine tag too, or every report over the attribution log under-counts the partner's work.
  55:     # Parsers reading pm/dev are unaffected; the attribute appears only when a partner rides.
  56:     # The short session tag reaches the machine line too (additive, founder 2026-08-11), so the
  57:     # attribution log can group prompts by session without parsing the human lines.
  58:     $sess = ''
  59:     if ($script:SessionId -and $script:SessionId.Length -ge 8) { $sess = (' session="' + $script:SessionId.Substring(0, 8) + '"') }
  60:     if ($script:BatchAttr) {
  61:         Write-Output ('<ai4good-attribution pm="{0}" dev="{1}" batch="{2}"{3}/>' -f $pm, $dev, $script:BatchAttr, $sess)
  62:     }
  63:     else {
  64:         Write-Output ('<ai4good-attribution pm="{0}" dev="{1}"{2}/>' -f $pm, $dev, $sess)
  65:     }
  66:     foreach ($e in $extra) { if ($e) { Write-Output ($pfx + $e) } }
  67:     foreach ($a in $script:AgentLines) { if ($a) { Write-Output ($pfx + $a) } }
  68: }
  69: 
  70: function Fmt([string]$id, [string]$label) {
  71:     if (-not $label) { return $id }
  72:     $t = ($label -replace '[\r\n\|<>"()]', ' ').Trim()
  73:     if ($t.Length -gt 40) { $t = $t.Substring(0, 37) + '...' }
  74:     if (-not $t) { return $id }
  75:     return ('{0} ({1})' -f $id, $t)
  76: }
  77: 
  78: # Strict ASCII token boundaries and zero-padding normalised, so `fix/notai4dev-19x` does not
  79: # match and `AI4DEV-019` does. ALL matches are collected: exactly one attributes, and two or more
  80: # is unresolved rather than "take the first" - `ai4dev-19-into-ai4dev-20` silently attributed to
  81: # 19 under the single anchored pattern this replaces.
  82: # CultureInvariant because `(?i)` under a Turkish locale does not fold `I` the way ASCII expects -
  83: # it would fail plain AI4DEV-19 and accept dotted-I lookalikes. `[0-9]` not `\d`, because .NET's
  84: # `\d` also matches Arabic-Indic and other Unicode digits, which are not Linear ids. A match
  85: # timeout because `0*[0-9]+` can backtrack badly on a long run of zeros, and a hook that hangs is
  86: # strictly worse than a hook that degrades.
  87: function Get-BranchItems([string]$branch) {
  88:     if ($branch.Length -gt 512) { return @() }
  89:     $rx = New-Object System.Text.RegularExpressions.Regex(
  90:         '(?<![\p{L}\p{N}])AI4(DEV|PM)-0*([0-9]{1,7})(?![\p{L}\p{N}])',
  91:         ([System.Text.RegularExpressions.RegexOptions]::IgnoreCase -bor [System.Text.RegularExpressions.RegexOptions]::CultureInvariant),
  92:         ([TimeSpan]::FromMilliseconds(250)))
  93:     $ids = New-Object System.Collections.ArrayList
  94:     foreach ($m in $rx.Matches($branch)) {
  95:         $id = 'AI4' + $m.Groups[1].Value.ToUpperInvariant() + '-' + [string][int]$m.Groups[2].Value
  96:         if ($ids -notcontains $id) { [void]$ids.Add($id) }
  97:     }
  98:     return $ids.ToArray()
  99: }
 100: 
 101: try {
 102:     # State paths come from work-lib so the hook and /work can never disagree about where the
 103:     # held item and the chain cache live. Two formulas for one path is the same drift class this
 104:     # item exists to delete.
 105:     . (Join-Path $PSScriptRoot 'work-lib.ps1')
 106: 
 107:     # The calling SESSION's identity, from the hook payload on stdin. Only read when stdin is
 108:     # genuinely redirected - reading a console would block, and a hook that hangs is the one
 109:     # failure this file must never have. Empty is a legal answer and degrades the ownership
 110:     # labels below, never the stamp.
 111:     # CAPTURE THE PARAMETER BEFORE ZEROING: at script level $script:SessionId IS $SessionId -
 112:     # the same variable - so assigning '' first would silently erase the banner's parameter
 113:     # (caught by test on 2026-08-12, not by inspection).
 114:     $sidParam = $SessionId
 115:     $script:SessionId = ''
 116:     if ($sidParam -and $sidParam -match '^[0-9a-fA-F-]{8,64}$') { $script:SessionId = $sidParam }
 117:     try {
 118:         if (-not $script:SessionId -and [Console]::IsInputRedirected) {
 119:             $raw = [Console]::In.ReadToEnd()
 120:             $m = [regex]::Match($raw, '"session_id"\s*:\s*"([0-9a-fA-F-]{8,64})"')
 121:             if ($m.Success) { $script:SessionId = $m.Groups[1].Value }
 122:         }
 123:     }
 124:     catch { }
 125: 
 126:     # -LiteralPath: a legitimate directory containing [ or ] is treated as a wildcard by Test-Path
 127:     # and reports false, silently falling back to some other directory's attribution.
 128:     $base = if ($env:CLAUDE_PROJECT_DIR -and (Test-Path -LiteralPath $env:CLAUDE_PROJECT_DIR)) { $env:CLAUDE_PROJECT_DIR } else { (Get-Location).Path }
 129: 
 130:     # Identity is git's, not the path string's. Windows case-folding and junctions make paths lie
 131:     # in both directions, so the worktree is identified by what git resolves, never by text.
 132:     $top = (& git -C $base rev-parse --show-toplevel 2>$null)
 133:     $gitDir = (& git -C $base rev-parse --git-dir 2>$null)
 134:     if (-not $top -or -not $gitDir) {
 135:         Emit 'unknown (stamp error: not a git worktree)' ('wt ? - ' + $base) @() '-' '-'
 136:         exit 0
 137:     }
 138:     $top = $top.Trim(); $gitDir = $gitDir.Trim()
 139:     if (-not [System.IO.Path]::IsPathRooted($gitDir)) { $gitDir = Join-Path $top $gitDir }
 140:     $wt = Split-Path -Leaf $top
 141:     $extra = @()
 142: 
 143:     # SESSION FRESHNESS GUARD (founder 2026-08-11). A session loads CLAUDE.md and the agent
 144:     # contracts ONCE, at start; disk edits after that are invisible to it, and spawns serve the
 145:     # registry's old bodies (adds never reload; edits lag). This session's first prompt records a
 146:     # fingerprint of those files; every later prompt compares, and drift prints HERE — the
 147:     # earliest point in any turn, in the one channel always read. Baseline is per session id, so
 148:     # every session in the folder gets its own honest answer. No session id (child re-runs,
 149:     # manual runs) → skip silently. Any failure degrades to a named note, never a block.
 150:     try {
 151:         if ($script:SessionId) {
 152:             $watched = @()
 153:             $cm = Join-Path $top 'CLAUDE.md'
 154:             if (Test-Path -LiteralPath $cm) { $watched += $cm }
 155:             $agentsDir = Join-Path $top '.claude\agents'
 156:             if (Test-Path -LiteralPath $agentsDir) {
 157:                 $watched += (Get-ChildItem -LiteralPath $agentsDir -Filter '*.md' -File | ForEach-Object { $_.FullName })
 158:             }
 159:             $sha = [System.Security.Cryptography.SHA256]::Create()
 160:             $now = @{}
 161:             foreach ($f in $watched) {
 162:                 $h = [System.BitConverter]::ToString($sha.ComputeHash([System.IO.File]::ReadAllBytes($f))) -replace '-', ''
 163:                 $now[(Split-Path -Leaf $f)] = $h.Substring(0, 16).ToLower()
 164:             }
 165:             # Session-bound (founder 2026-08-11): the baseline lives in THIS session's own cache
 166:             # directory, beside its held label — one directory per session, keyed by the id the
 167:             # hook payload carries.
 168:             $wfile = Join-Path (Get-StateDirRO) ('sessions\' + $script:SessionId + '\watch.json')
 169:             $watchBaseline = Read-JsonCapped $wfile
 170:             if (-not $watchBaseline) {
 171:                 [System.IO.File]::WriteAllText((Join-Path (Get-SessionStateDir $script:SessionId) 'watch.json'), (@{ files = $now; recordedAt = (Get-Date).ToUniversalTime().ToString('o') } | ConvertTo-Json -Compress), (New-Object System.Text.UTF8Encoding($false)))
 172:             }
 173:             else {
 174:                 $drifted = @()
 175:                 foreach ($k in $now.Keys) {
 176:                     $old = $null
 177:                     try { $old = [string]$watchBaseline.files.$k } catch { }
 178:                     if ($old -and $old -ne $now[$k]) { $drifted += $k }
 179:                     elseif (-not $old) { $drifted += ($k + ' (new)') }
 180:                 }
 181:                 if ($drifted.Count -gt 0) {
 182:                     $extra += ('CONTRACTS DRIFTED since this session started: ' + ($drifted -join ', ') + ' - this session and its running agents keep the OLD text, and new spawns may too (adds never reload; edits lag). Restart before the next spawn, or probe the changed type live.')
 183:                 }
 184:             }
 185:         }
 186:     }
 187:     catch {
 188:         $extra += ('freshness guard degraded: ' + ($_.Exception.Message -replace '[\r\n]', ' '))
 189:     }
 190:     # THE WINDOW ALARM (founder line 2026-08-12: 85 percent). The founder is the actor who can act
 191:     # on a spent window - park the running work, wait, restart it - so the founder-facing channel
 192:     # is this stamp, on every prompt, for as long as the condition lasts.
 193:     #
 194:     # IT COMPUTES, IT DOES NOT READ THE ONE-LINE VERDICT FILE. The per-tool alarm reads that line
 195:     # because it is paid on every tool call; this runs once per prompt, beside several git calls,
 196:     # so the cost of computing is nothing. And it must compute: a raw read would echo an hours-old
 197:     # OK if the sensor had died, and the founder channel is exactly the one that must always apply
 198:     # the staleness rules rather than repeat a frozen answer.
 199:     #
 200:     # Degraded is a NOTE, never a halt: UNKNOWN reports loudly and does not stop work, the same
 201:     # rule attribution follows. The dot-source is guarded so a missing or broken library costs a
 202:     # named line, not the stamp.
 203:     try {
 204:         . (Join-Path $PSScriptRoot 'window-lib.ps1')
 205:         $wv = Get-WindowVerdict
 206:         if ($wv.verdict -eq 'PAUSE') {
 207:             # One composition, in the library. The leading ALARM token exists for the per-tool
 208:             # checkbox to anchor on with findstr; here the founder-facing prefix is the anchor, so
 209:             # the token is dropped rather than printed twice.
 210:             $extra += ('WINDOW ALARM  ' + ((Format-WindowVerdictLine $wv) -replace '^ALARM WINDOW ', ''))
 211:         }
 212:         elseif ($wv.verdict -eq 'UNKNOWN') {
 213:             $extra += ('window sensor cannot be trusted, not halting: ' + (([string]$wv.reason) -replace '[\r\n]', ' '))
 214:         }
 215:     }
 216:     catch {
 217:         $extra += ('window check degraded: ' + ($_.Exception.Message -replace '[\r\n]', ' '))
 218:     }
 219: 
 220:     $script:Actor = if ($top -match '[\\/]\.claude[\\/]worktrees[\\/]') { 'AGENT' } else { 'COORDINATOR' }
 221: 
 222:     # SUPERVISED AGENTS (founder 2026-08-05). From the MAIN checkout, every platform agent
 223:     # worktree gets its own derived stamp appended, so the founder sees the whole supervision
 224:     # tree on every prompt without asking. Each agent line is this hook re-run against that
 225:     # worktree - ONE derivation logic, never a second formula that could drift. An agent
 226:     # worktree itself skips this block (its stamp IS the agent line), which also terminates
 227:     # the recursion; the path guard matches both slash directions because git reports forward
 228:     # slashes on Windows.
 229:     try {
 230:         $wtRoot = Join-Path $top '.claude/worktrees'
 231:         if ((-not $env:AI4GOOD_STAMP_CHILD) -and ($top -notmatch '[\\/]\.claude[\\/]worktrees[\\/]') -and (Test-Path -LiteralPath $wtRoot)) {
 232:             $savedEnv = $env:CLAUDE_PROJECT_DIR
 233:             $env:AI4GOOD_STAMP_CHILD = '1'
 234:             foreach ($d in (Get-ChildItem -LiteralPath $wtRoot -Directory -ErrorAction SilentlyContinue)) {
 235:                 # ONLY a real worktree is recursed into. `.claude/worktrees` also holds ordinary
 236:                 # directories - the artifacts directory the conductor keeps per item - and those
 237:                 # are INSIDE this repository, so git answers with the MAIN checkout's toplevel.
 238:                 # The old guard tested that answer, saw the main checkout, and enumerated this
 239:                 # directory again: unbounded self-spawning that hung the hook for minutes and
 240:                 # printed nothing. A stamp that does not appear is the one failure this file
 241:                 # exists to prevent, so the test is now "is this directory itself a worktree",
 242:                 # and AI4GOOD_STAMP_CHILD makes any future path incapable of recursing at all.
 243:                 $childTop = (& git -C $d.FullName rev-parse --show-toplevel 2>$null)
 244:                 if (-not $childTop) { continue }
 245:                 $a = [System.IO.Path]::GetFullPath(($childTop.Trim() -replace '/', '\')).TrimEnd('\')
 246:                 $b = [System.IO.Path]::GetFullPath($d.FullName).TrimEnd('\')
 247:                 if (-not $a.Equals($b, [System.StringComparison]::OrdinalIgnoreCase)) { continue }
 248: 
 249:                 $env:CLAUDE_PROJECT_DIR = $d.FullName
 250:                 $child = @(& powershell -NoProfile -File $PSCommandPath 2>$null)
 251:                 if ($child.Count -ge 2) {
 252:                     # Verbatim, plus an OWNERSHIP tag (founder 2026-08-09). These lines print in
 253:                     # EVERY session opened in this folder - the tree is derived from disk - so a
 254:                     # parallel session reads them as its own agents unless each line says whose
 255:                     # agent it is. The owner is recorded by /work at spawn; absent records and an
 256:                     # unknown session id both degrade to words, never to a guess.
 257:                     # A DIFFERENT SPAWNER ID IS NOT ANOTHER SESSION (founder catch 2026-08-12).
 258:                     # The founder's one thread of work can span several session ids: /clear
 259:                     # always mints a new id, resume can fork onto one, and a resume aimed at an
 260:                     # explicit id can land on an OLDER trunk than the session it meant to
 261:                     # continue (measured 2026-08-12: a stale dispatch pointer did exactly that,
 262:                     # and agents spawned in the post-clear session read as id mismatches in the
 263:                     # resumed trunk). The old wording asserted "ANOTHER session's agent" on any
 264:                     # mismatch, and the coordinator believed it over the founder's own account.
 265:                     # No repair is derivable: the hook payload names no predecessor id and
 266:                     # transcripts carry only their own id. So the label states exactly what the
 267:                     # comparison knows - the spawner's tag, and the mismatch's readings - and
 268:                     # the reader resolves it by asking or from conversation memory.
 269:                     $who = 'spawner unrecorded - not necessarily this session''s'
 270:                     try {
 271:                         # GIT'S OWN STRING, never $a. The worktree id is a hash of the toplevel
 272:                         # TEXT, so `C:/x` and `C:\x` hash differently: $a is the backslash form
 273:                         # built for the path-equality test above, and passing it here missed a
 274:                         # record that existed and printed "spawner unrecorded" for my own agent
 275:                         # (measured 2026-08-09, first live use). Every writer uses git's forward-
 276:                         # slash output, so every reader must too.
 277:                         $own = Get-OwnerForWorktreeRoot ($childTop.Trim())
 278:                         $ownSid = if ($own) { [string]$own.sessionId } else { '' }
 279:                         if ($ownSid -and $script:SessionId) {
 280:                             if ($ownSid -eq $script:SessionId) { $who = 'this session''s agent' }
 281:                             else {
 282:                                 $ownTag = if ($ownSid.Length -ge 8) { $ownSid.Substring(0, 8) } else { $ownSid }
 283:                                 $who = ('spawner session ' + $ownTag + ' - another session, or this one before a resume')
 284:                             }
 285:                         }
 286:                         elseif ($ownSid) { $who = 'spawner recorded; this session''s id unknown' }
 287:                     }
 288:                     catch { }
 289:                     $script:AgentLines += ([string]$child[0] + '   [' + $who + ']')
 290:                     $script:AgentLines += ([string]$child[1])
 291:                     # AND ITS WARNINGS. Forwarding only the first two lines silently dropped every
 292:                     # qualifier the child raised - STALE, CHAIN UNRESOLVED, BRANCH NAMES 2 ITEMS,
 293:                     # an ignored held item. An agent whose branch named two items therefore showed
 294:                     # as "WORKING ON nothing" in the supervision tree, indistinguishable from one
 295:                     # that simply had not switched branch yet - and that exact display was on
 296:                     # screen this morning for an innocent reason, which is what makes the
 297:                     # ambiguity dangerous. A warning nobody can see is the failure this file
 298:                     # exists to prevent. The machine tag is the child's own and is not repeated.
 299:                     for ($i = 2; $i -lt $child.Count; $i++) {
 300:                         $w = [string]$child[$i]
 301:                         if ($w -and ($w -notmatch '^<ai4good-attribution')) { $script:AgentLines += ('            ' + $w) }
 302:                     }
 303:                 }
 304:             }
 305:             $env:CLAUDE_PROJECT_DIR = $savedEnv
 306:             $env:AI4GOOD_STAMP_CHILD = $null
 307:         }
 308:     }
 309:     catch {
 310:         # Never silent, never fatal: a broken enumeration names itself and the main stamp stands.
 311:         $script:AgentLines += ('AGENT       (enumeration failed: ' + ($_.Exception.Message -replace '[\r\n]', ' ') + ')')
 312:     }
 313: 
 314:     # Named git states. The earlier design printed "nothing" during a rebase or a bisect - hours of
 315:     # genuinely attributed work reported as unattributed, which teaches you to ignore the line.
 316:     if ((Test-Path (Join-Path $gitDir 'rebase-merge')) -or (Test-Path (Join-Path $gitDir 'rebase-apply'))) {
 317:         $extra += 'REBASE IN PROGRESS - closure blocked until it finishes'
 318:     }
 319:     if (Test-Path (Join-Path $gitDir 'BISECT_LOG')) {
 320:         $extra += 'BISECT IN PROGRESS - closure blocked until it finishes'
 321:     }
 322: 
 323:     $branch = (& git -C $base symbolic-ref --short -q HEAD 2>$null)
 324:     $detached = $false
 325:     if ($branch) { $branch = $branch.Trim() } else {
 326:         $detached = $true
 327:         $sha = (& git -C $base rev-parse --short HEAD 2>$null)
 328:         $branch = if ($sha) { 'detached@' + $sha.Trim() } else { 'detached@unknown' }
 329:     }
 330: 
 331:     # The held item - a cross-check only. Keyed by repo root: two sessions in one folder share it,
 332:     # which can produce a spurious CONFLICT but can never produce a wrong attribution, because it
 333:     # is never the answer.
 334:     # Held state is only usable as attribution if it proves it was taken up ON THIS BRANCH. Without
 335:     # that, taking up an item and later switching to main would keep claiming the old item forever.
 336:     # An id that fails validation is discarded outright: one containing newlines would forge extra
 337:     # stamp lines and a fake attribution tag - the very output the founder is told to trust.
 338:     $held = $null
 339:     try { $held = Get-HeldItem -SessionId $script:SessionId } catch { }
 340:     $heldId = ''
 341:     $heldStale = ''
 342:     if ($held -and ((Test-ItemId ([string]$held.itemId)) -or (Test-FloatingRoot ([string]$held.itemId)))) {
 343:         if (([string]$held.branch) -eq $branch) { $heldId = [string]$held.itemId }
 344:         else { $heldStale = [string]$held.itemId }
 345:     }
 346: 
 347:     $ids = @(Get-BranchItems $branch)
 348:     $item = ''
 349:     if ($ids.Count -eq 1) { $item = $ids[0] }
 350:     elseif ($ids.Count -gt 1) {
 351:         $extra += ('BRANCH NAMES ' + $ids.Count + ' ITEMS (' + ($ids -join ', ') + ') - unresolved; rename the branch or say which')
 352:     }
 353: 
 354:     # "folder", not "wt": an abbreviation nobody outside this repository can expand is exactly the
 355:     # shorthand the founder's standing instruction forbids, and this line is read by the founder.
 356:     # The SHORT SESSION TAG (founder 2026-08-11: "I want a hash or something unique that can
 357:     # identify the session... short since session ids are long"): the first 8 characters of the
 358:     # session id — already random hex, so no hashing is needed, and the founder can match it to
 359:     # a window at a glance. A new tag after a compact or resume is a FEATURE: identity rotation
 360:     # becomes visible instead of silent. Child re-runs carry no session id and print no tag.
 361:     $line2 = ('folder {0} - branch {1}' -f $wt, $branch)
 362:     if ($script:SessionId -and $script:SessionId.Length -ge 8) {
 363:         $line2 += (' - session ' + $script:SessionId.Substring(0, 8))
 364:     }
 365: 
 366:     # CONFLICT outranks everything. Never pick a side: exactly one of the two describes work that
 367:     # is not happening, and that is the condition the old design could never see.
 368:     if ($item -and $heldId -and $item -ne $heldId) {
 369:         Emit ('CONFLICT - branch says ' + $item + ', session holds ' + $heldId) $line2 `
 370:         ($extra + 'Attribution unresolved until you say which is right. Building is not blocked; closing is.') 'conflict' 'conflict'
 371:         exit 0
 372:     }
 373: 
 374:     # Branch names nothing, but the session took an item up ON THIS BRANCH: fill the gap, and say
 375:     # it is a gap. This is also how COORDINATOR LIGHT WORK attributes (founder 2026-08-09): work
 376:     # ruled onto main outside an item records a held item, and this line then carries it.
 377:     $heldFill = $false
 378:     $heldLabel = ''
 379:     if (-not $item -and $heldId -and $ids.Count -eq 0) {
 380:         $extra += ('held, not branch - the branch names no item' + $(if ($detached) { ' (detached HEAD)' } else { '' }))
 381:         $item = $heldId
 382:         $heldFill = $true
 383:         if ($held) { $heldLabel = [string]$held.label }
 384:     }
 385:     if ($heldStale) {
 386:         $extra += ('IGNORING held ' + $heldStale + ' - it was taken up on another branch; not attributing to it')
 387:     }
 388: 
 389:     if (-not $item) {
 390:         # "nothing" was misleading (founder 2026-08-09): the coordinator is always DOING
 391:         # something - board, relays, sweeps, folds - it just claims no ITEM. So the coordinator's
 392:         # idle line names its standing work instead of an absence, and the nudge tells it how to
 393:         # attribute item-scoped light work: hold the item. An AGENT keeps the bare wording - it
 394:         # was spawned for one item, and "coordination" would be false there.
 395:         if ($script:Actor -eq 'AGENT') {
 396:             Emit 'nothing - this branch names no item yet' $line2 $extra 'none' 'none'
 397:         }
 398:         elseif ($script:AgentLines.Count -gt 0) {
 399:             Emit 'coordination, no item claimed here - the items below run in their own folders' $line2 $extra 'none' 'none'
 400:         }
 401:         else {
 402:             Emit 'coordination, no item claimed - board, relays, sweeps and folds happen here' $line2 ($extra + 'item-scoped light work? hold the item so this line attributes it; exploring? say so') 'none' 'none'
 403:         }
 404:         exit 0
 405:     }
 406: 
 407:     # The chain, from cache. Keyed by worktree id + branch, so two clones never share an entry
 408:     # and a branch renamed into a previously-cached name misses instead of hitting.
 409:     $chain = $null
 410:     try { $chain = Get-Chain $branch } catch { }
 411: 
 412:     # An unresolved chain claims NO root. The earlier version fell back to the item itself, which
 413:     # printed pm="AI4DEV-36" - asserting an item is its own root when the parent is simply unknown.
 414:     # Unknown is a value; inventing one is the failure this whole design exists to prevent.
 415:     $root = '-'
 416:     if (Test-Chain $chain $branch $item) {
 417:         $nodes = @($chain.chain)
 418:         $parts = @()
 419:         foreach ($n in $nodes) { $parts += (Fmt ([string]$n.id) ([string]$n.label)) }
 420:         $line1 = ($parts -join ' > ')
 421:         $root = [string]$nodes[0].id
 422:         $fresh = $false
 423:         try {
 424:             $age = (Get-Date) - [datetime]::Parse([string]$chain.resolvedAt, [Globalization.CultureInfo]::InvariantCulture)
 425:             $fresh = ($age.TotalHours -le 24 -and $age.TotalMinutes -ge -5)   # a future stamp is not "fresh"
 426:         }
 427:         catch { }
 428:         if (-not $fresh) { $extra += 'STALE - cached chain is old or its timestamp is unreadable; /work refreshes it' }
 429:         # A batch partner rides this branch (batching mode, founder 2026-08-11). The stamp says
 430:         # so, or two items run while the tree shows one.
 431:         try {
 432:             if ($chain.batchedWith -and (Test-ItemId ([string]$chain.batchedWith.id))) {
 433:                 $extra += ('batched with ' + (Fmt ([string]$chain.batchedWith.id) ([string]$chain.batchedWith.label)) + ' - closes via its sanctioned line in this branch''s pull request')
 434:                 $script:BatchAttr = [string]$chain.batchedWith.id
 435:             }
 436:         }
 437:         catch { }
 438:     }
 439:     elseif ($heldFill) {
 440:         # Held attribution has no branch-keyed chain by design - the branch is main. The held
 441:         # label makes the line legible, and the "held, not branch" note above already says why
 442:         # there are no parents; adding CHAIN UNRESOLVED here would flag normal light work as a
 443:         # defect on every prompt.
 444:         $line1 = Fmt $item $heldLabel
 445:     }
 446:     else {
 447:         $line1 = $item
 448:         $extra += 'CHAIN UNRESOLVED - no valid cached parents for this branch; /work resolves it'
 449:     }
 450: 
 451:     Emit $line1 $line2 $extra $root $item
 452: }
 453: catch {
 454:     Write-Output ('WORKING ON  unknown (stamp error: ' + ($_.Exception.Message -replace '[\r\n]', ' ') + ')')
 455:     Write-Output 'IN          wt ? - branch ?'
 456:     Write-Output '<ai4good-attribution pm="-" dev="-"/>'
 457: }

===== loop/work/window-wait.ps1 =====
   1: # window-wait.ps1 - block until the usage window has reopened, then exit 0.
   2: #
   3: # HOW IT IS MEANT TO BE USED. Run it as a BACKGROUND command. Its exit re-invokes the session,
   4: # which is the whole trick: the coordinator parks, this waits without spending anything, and the
   5: # founder's session continues in the same conversation with its context intact. Nothing is
   6: # resumed, nothing is restarted, no scheduled task is involved.
   7: #
   8: # WHY IT WAITS ON THE CLOCK AND NOT ON THE GAUGE. The gauge is refreshed by the status line, and
   9: # the status line only renders when the session takes a turn. A parked session takes no turns -
  10: # so during exactly the period this script is waiting, the reading it would like to poll is
  11: # frozen. Polling it alone would wait forever on a number that cannot change.
  12: #
  13: # So the wait is anchored on `resets_at`, which the provider states and which is true whether or
  14: # not anyone is looking. The gauge is still read every poll, because if some OTHER session in the
  15: # account renders its status line the snapshot updates and we can leave early - but the clock is
  16: # what guarantees an exit.
  17: #
  18: # THIS SCRIPT CANNOT CLEAR THE WINDOW, ONLY OBSERVE THAT IT SHOULD HAVE. Its exit means "the
  19: # stated reset time has passed", not "you have budget". The work skill already warns why that
  20: # distinction bites: a reset time that is already past rolls forward a full day, and a session
  21: # limit is not the same thing as being out of credit. **The caller MUST re-read the gauge on its
  22: # first turn after this exits** - that turn renders the status line, so it is the first genuinely
  23: # fresh reading available - and park again if it still says PAUSE.
  24: #
  25: # Exit 0: the window should be open, go and confirm. Exit 1: gave up (see -MaxHours), which is a
  26: # report to the founder, never a licence to carry on regardless.
  27: 
  28: [CmdletBinding()]
  29: param(
  30:     # How often to look. A parked session is spending nothing, so there is no reason to poll
  31:     # hard; a minute is far below the resolution of a five-hour window.
  32:     [int]$PollSeconds = 60,
  33: 
  34:     # Refuse to wait longer than this. A five-hour window cannot need more, so exceeding it means
  35:     # the blocking window is a WEEKLY one - days away, which is a founder decision about what to
  36:     # do next, not something a script should sit through silently.
  37:     [double]$MaxHours = 6,
  38: 
  39:     # Passed through, so a caller testing the mechanism can move the line without editing code.
  40:     # The default is the founder's number (2026-08-12): 85 percent, superseding the 90 of
  41:     # 2026-08-06. Only the number moved; one line still means alarm and park together. It matches
  42:     # window-gauge.ps1 and window-lib.ps1, and the three move together.
  43:     [int]$PauseAt = 85,
  44: 
  45:     # Passed through: exercise the park against a synthetic snapshot, spending nothing.
  46:     [string]$SnapshotPath = '',
  47: 
  48:     # Grace past the stated reset before releasing. A reset is a boundary the provider crosses,
  49:     # not an instant we control, so releasing on the exact second risks arriving early. Only the
  50:     # simulation sets this to zero, to cross a boundary in seconds instead of a minute.
  51:     [double]$SlackMinutes = 1
  52: )
  53: 
  54: $ErrorActionPreference = 'SilentlyContinue'
  55: $gauge = Join-Path $PSScriptRoot 'window-gauge.ps1'
  56: 
  57: function Read-Gauge {
  58:     try {
  59:         $a = @('-NoProfile','-ExecutionPolicy','Bypass','-File',$gauge,'-Json','-PauseAt',$PauseAt)
  60:         if ($SnapshotPath) { $a += @('-SnapshotPath', $SnapshotPath) }
  61:         $raw = & powershell @a
  62:         return ($raw | ConvertFrom-Json)
  63:     } catch { return $null }
  64: }
  65: 
  66: $started  = Get-Date
  67: $deadline = $started.AddHours($MaxHours)
  68: 
  69: $g = Read-Gauge
  70: if (-not $g) {
  71:     Write-Output 'WAIT  abandoned - the gauge could not be read at all'
  72:     exit 1
  73: }
  74: 
  75: if ($g.verdict -eq 'OK') {
  76:     Write-Output ("WAIT  nothing to wait for - " + $g.reason)
  77:     exit 0
  78: }
  79: 
  80: # The target comes from the provider's own statement of when this window resets. A minute of
  81: # slack because the reset is a boundary, not an instant we control.
  82: $target = $null
  83: if ($g.resetsAtUtc) { try { $target = ([datetime]::Parse($g.resetsAtUtc)).ToUniversalTime().AddMinutes($SlackMinutes) } catch { } }
  84: if (-not $target) {
  85:     Write-Output 'WAIT  abandoned - no reset time in the reading, so there is nothing to wait for'
  86:     exit 1
  87: }
  88: 
  89: Write-Output ("WAIT  parked on {0} at {1}% - reset stated for {2} local" -f `
  90:     $g.worstWindow, $g.worstPercent, ([datetime]$target).ToLocalTime().ToString('HH:mm'))
  91: 
  92: $lastSaid = Get-Date
  93: while ($true) {
  94:     $now = (Get-Date).ToUniversalTime()
  95: 
  96:     if ($now -ge $target) {
  97:         Write-Output 'WAIT  the stated reset time has passed - re-read the gauge before releasing work'
  98:         exit 0
  99:     }
 100: 
 101:     if ((Get-Date) -ge $deadline) {
 102:         Write-Output ("WAIT  gave up after {0}h - the blocking window is {1}, which resets {2} local. This is a founder decision, not something to wait out." -f `
 103:             $MaxHours, $g.worstWindow, ([datetime]$target).ToLocalTime().ToString('ddd HH:mm'))
 104:         exit 1
 105:     }
 106: 
 107:     # Another session in the account may have rendered its status line, which refreshes the shared
 108:     # snapshot. If it now reads clear, there is no reason to keep sitting here.
 109:     $fresh = Read-Gauge
 110:     if ($fresh -and $fresh.verdict -eq 'OK') {
 111:         Write-Output ("WAIT  cleared early - " + $fresh.reason)
 112:         exit 0
 113:     }
 114: 
 115:     # Say something every ten minutes. Silence and progress look identical, and a wait that never
 116:     # speaks is indistinguishable from a wait that died.
 117:     if (((Get-Date) - $lastSaid).TotalMinutes -ge 10) {
 118:         $left = [int]([math]::Ceiling(($target - $now).TotalMinutes))
 119:         Write-Output ("WAIT  still parked - about {0} min to the stated reset" -f $left)
 120:         $lastSaid = Get-Date
 121:     }
 122: 
 123:     Start-Sleep -Seconds $PollSeconds
 124: }

===== loop/work/window-sim.ps1 =====
   1: # window-sim.ps1 - exercise the usage-window guard end to end, spending nothing.
   2: #
   3: # The whole mechanism reads exactly ONE file, so driving that file drives everything: no API
   4: # calls, no tokens, no waiting for a real window to fill. Every reading here is synthetic and
   5: # written to a scratch path, so the live snapshot the status line maintains is never touched.
   6: #
   7: # WHAT THIS CAN AND CANNOT PROVE. It proves the parts we wrote behave: the gauge's verdicts, the
   8: # park's exits, the early release. It does NOT prove the sensor is reading Anthropic correctly -
   9: # the synthetic readings are shaped the way we BELIEVE the real one is shaped, so a change in the
  10: # provider's payload would sail straight through this green. That gap is covered by the live
  11: # reading, not here.
  12: #
  13: # It found a real bug on its first run: the gauge picked the binding window with Sort-Object over
  14: # dictionary entries, which silently does nothing, so "worst" was whichever enumerated first. The
  15: # live reading agreed with the right answer by luck for weeks. Cases that can DISAGREE are the
  16: # whole point of this file - a test that only reproduces the situation you already have proves
  17: # nothing.
  18: #
  19: # Run: powershell -NoProfile -File loop/work/window-sim.ps1
  20: 
  21: $ErrorActionPreference = 'SilentlyContinue'
  22: $here  = $PSScriptRoot
  23: $gauge = Join-Path $here 'window-gauge.ps1'
  24: $wait  = Join-Path $here 'window-wait.ps1'
  25: $snap  = Join-Path $env:TEMP ('window-sim-' + [guid]::NewGuid().ToString('N').Substring(0,8) + '.json')
  26: 
  27: $pass = 0; $fail = 0; $failed = @()
  28: 
  29: function Set-Reading([hashtable]$windows, [double]$ageMinutes = 0) {
  30:     $o = [ordered]@{
  31:         capturedAt = (Get-Date).ToUniversalTime().AddMinutes(-$ageMinutes).ToString('o')
  32:         sessionId  = 'sim'; version = 'sim'
  33:         rateLimits = $windows
  34:     }
  35:     [IO.File]::WriteAllText($snap, ($o | ConvertTo-Json -Depth 6))
  36: }
  37: function Set-Raw([string]$text) { [IO.File]::WriteAllText($snap, $text) }
  38: 
  39: # A window: percent used, and how many minutes until it resets.
  40: function W($pct, [double]$resetInMinutes) {
  41:     @{ used_percentage = $pct; resets_at = [DateTimeOffset]::UtcNow.AddMinutes($resetInMinutes).ToUnixTimeSeconds() }
  42: }
  43: 
  44: function Check([string]$label, $got, $want) {
  45:     if ("$got" -eq "$want") { $script:pass++; "  PASS  {0}" -f $label }
  46:     else { $script:fail++; $script:failed += $label; "  FAIL  {0}  (got '{1}', wanted '{2}')" -f $label, $got, $want }
  47: }
  48: 
  49: function Gauge() {
  50:     & powershell -NoProfile -ExecutionPolicy Bypass -File $gauge -Json -SnapshotPath $snap | ConvertFrom-Json
  51: }
  52: function Verdict() { (Gauge).verdict }
  53: 
  54: try {
  55:     'SIMULATING THE USAGE-WINDOW GUARD (synthetic readings, no tokens spent)'
  56:     ''
  57:     '1. A five-hour window filling up across a working session'
  58:     # The narrative the guard exists for: work proceeds, spend climbs, and at one specific
  59:     # reading the answer changes. Nothing else about the situation changes at that moment.
  60:     foreach ($step in @(3, 25, 60, 80, 84)) {
  61:         Set-Reading @{ five_hour = (W $step 120); seven_day = (W 30 5000) }
  62:         Check ("at {0}% -> keep working" -f $step) (Verdict) 'OK'
  63:     }
  64:     foreach ($step in @(85, 91, 99, 100)) {
  65:         Set-Reading @{ five_hour = (W $step 120); seven_day = (W 30 5000) }
  66:         Check ("at {0}% -> park" -f $step) (Verdict) 'PAUSE'
  67:     }
  68: 
  69:     ''
  70:     '2. The line itself'
  71:     # The line is 85 (founder 2026-08-12, superseding 90 of 2026-08-06). These four cases are what
  72:     # pins it: a line half-moved between the gauge, the wait and the library goes red here.
  73:     Set-Reading @{ five_hour = (W 84 60) }
  74:     Check '84 is under the line' (Verdict) 'OK'
  75:     Set-Reading @{ five_hour = (W 85 60) }
  76:     Check '85 is ON the line and counts as over' (Verdict) 'PAUSE'
  77:     # The provider sends floats carrying binary noise; the gauge rounds before comparing. Without
  78:     # that, the boundary behaves differently depending on invisible digits.
  79:     Set-Reading @{ five_hour = (W 84.6 60) }
  80:     Check '84.6 rounds to 85 -> park' (Verdict) 'PAUSE'
  81:     Set-Reading @{ five_hour = (W 84.4 60) }
  82:     Check '84.4 rounds to 84 -> keep working' (Verdict) 'OK'
  83:     Set-Reading @{ five_hour = (W 7.000000000000001 60) }
  84:     Check 'float noise does not become an odd percentage' (Gauge).worstPercent 7
  85:     Set-Reading @{ five_hour = (W 0 60) }
  86:     Check 'a fresh window reads 0' (Gauge).worstPercent 0
  87: 
  88:     ''
  89:     '3. Several windows, disagreeing'
  90:     Set-Reading @{ five_hour = (W 12 200); seven_day = (W 95 5000) }
  91:     Check 'weekly over while five-hour is idle -> park' (Verdict) 'PAUSE'
  92:     Check 'and it names the weekly one' (Gauge).worstWindow 'seven_day'
  93:     # The reset it reports must belong to the window that BLOCKED, or a park would wait on the
  94:     # wrong clock - the five-hour reset here is 80 hours earlier than the weekly one.
  95:     Check 'and reports THAT window reset, not another' ([int]((Gauge).resetsInMin / 100)) 50
  96:     Set-Reading @{ five_hour = (W 96 30); seven_day = (W 91 5000) }
  97:     Check 'both over -> the furthest along wins' (Gauge).worstWindow 'five_hour'
  98:     # Other plans report up to five windows. Every one present is evaluated by name, so a plan
  99:     # change cannot silently narrow the guard to the two windows this account happens to have.
 100:     Set-Reading @{
 101:         five_hour = (W 10 100); seven_day = (W 20 5000)
 102:         seven_day_overage_included = (W 30 5000)
 103:         seven_day_sonnet = (W 40 5000); seven_day_opus = (W 93 5000)
 104:     }
 105:     Check 'a per-model weekly window blocks too' (Verdict) 'PAUSE'
 106:     Check 'and is named' (Gauge).worstWindow 'seven_day_opus'
 107:     # A window name nobody has seen before must still count. Ignoring the unknown is how a guard
 108:     # quietly stops covering the thing that was added.
 109:     Set-Reading @{ five_hour = (W 5 100); some_future_window = (W 97 999) }
 110:     Check 'an unrecognised window still counts' (Verdict) 'PAUSE'
 111: 
 112:     ''
 113:     '4. Readings that cannot be trusted'
 114:     Remove-Item $snap -Force
 115:     Check 'no snapshot at all -> UNKNOWN' (Verdict) 'UNKNOWN'
 116:     Set-Raw 'this is not json {{{'
 117:     Check 'unparseable snapshot -> UNKNOWN' (Verdict) 'UNKNOWN'
 118:     Set-Raw '{"capturedAt":"2026-08-07T00:00:00Z","rateLimits":null}'
 119:     Check 'a build reporting no windows -> UNKNOWN' (Verdict) 'UNKNOWN'
 120:     Set-Reading @{ five_hour = @{ resets_at = 123 } }
 121:     Check 'a window with no percentage -> UNKNOWN' (Verdict) 'UNKNOWN'
 122:     # Partial damage must not discard the windows that ARE readable.
 123:     Set-Reading @{ five_hour = @{ resets_at = 123 }; seven_day = (W 94 5000) }
 124:     Check 'one broken window, one good -> the good one still decides' (Verdict) 'PAUSE'
 125:     Set-Reading @{ five_hour = @{ used_percentage = 95 } }
 126:     Check 'a window with no reset time still reports its level' (Verdict) 'PAUSE'
 127:     Set-Raw ('{"capturedAt":"not-a-date","rateLimits":{"five_hour":{"used_percentage":95,"resets_at":' + [DateTimeOffset]::UtcNow.AddMinutes(60).ToUnixTimeSeconds() + '}}}')
 128:     Check 'an unreadable timestamp does not crash it' (Verdict) 'PAUSE'
 129: 
 130:     ''
 131:     '5. Staleness'
 132:     Set-Reading @{ five_hour = (W 50 100) } 0
 133:     Check 'a current reading is used' (Verdict) 'OK'
 134:     Set-Reading @{ five_hour = (W 50 100) } 14
 135:     Check '14 minutes old is still current enough' (Verdict) 'OK'
 136:     Set-Reading @{ five_hour = (W 50 100) } 16
 137:     Check '16 minutes old -> UNKNOWN' (Verdict) 'UNKNOWN'
 138:     # A stale reading proves a FLOOR, not a level - inside one window the figure only climbs. So
 139:     # staleness alone is not the question; whether that window has RESET since is.
 140:     Set-Reading @{ five_hour = (W 97 100) } 60
 141:     Check 'stale, over the line, window not yet reset -> park on it' (Verdict) 'PAUSE'
 142:     Set-Reading @{ five_hour = (W 97 -30) } 60
 143:     Check 'stale, over the line, but the window has since reset -> UNKNOWN' (Verdict) 'UNKNOWN'
 144:     Set-Reading @{ five_hour = (W 40 100) } 60
 145:     Check 'stale and under the line -> UNKNOWN, it may have climbed' (Verdict) 'UNKNOWN'
 146:     Set-Reading @{ five_hour = @{ used_percentage = 97 } } 60
 147:     Check 'stale, over the line, no reset time at all -> UNKNOWN' (Verdict) 'UNKNOWN'
 148: 
 149:     ''
 150:     '6. Parking and continuing'
 151:     Set-Reading @{ five_hour = (W 10 200) }
 152:     & powershell -NoProfile -ExecutionPolicy Bypass -File $wait -SnapshotPath $snap -PollSeconds 1 | Out-Null
 153:     Check 'nothing to wait for -> exits 0 at once' $LASTEXITCODE 0
 154:     & powershell -NoProfile -ExecutionPolicy Bypass -File $gauge -SnapshotPath $snap -ExitOnReady | Out-Null
 155:     Check 'the wait condition succeeds when clear' $LASTEXITCODE 0
 156:     Set-Reading @{ five_hour = (W 95 60) }
 157:     & powershell -NoProfile -ExecutionPolicy Bypass -File $gauge -SnapshotPath $snap -ExitOnReady | Out-Null
 158:     Check 'the wait condition fails when parked' $LASTEXITCODE 1
 159: 
 160:     Set-Reading @{ five_hour = (W 97 -3) }
 161:     $out = & powershell -NoProfile -ExecutionPolicy Bypass -File $wait -SnapshotPath $snap -PollSeconds 1
 162:     Check 'reset time already passed -> exits 0' $LASTEXITCODE 0
 163:     Check 'and tells the caller to re-read the gauge' ([bool]($out -match 're-read the gauge')) 'True'
 164: 
 165:     Set-Reading @{ seven_day = (W 96 4000) }
 166:     $out = & powershell -NoProfile -ExecutionPolicy Bypass -File $wait -SnapshotPath $snap -PollSeconds 1 -MaxHours 0
 167:     Check 'a weekly blocker -> gives up with exit 1' $LASTEXITCODE 1
 168:     Check 'and says it is a founder decision' ([bool]($out -match 'founder decision')) 'True'
 169: 
 170:     # Moving the line proves the threshold is a parameter and not baked into the logic.
 171:     Set-Reading @{ five_hour = (W 50 60) }
 172:     & powershell -NoProfile -ExecutionPolicy Bypass -File $wait -SnapshotPath $snap -PollSeconds 1 -PauseAt 40 -MaxHours 0 | Out-Null
 173:     Check 'a lowered line parks work that was fine before' $LASTEXITCODE 1
 174: 
 175:     ''
 176:     '7. A live park, with the world changing underneath it'
 177:     # This exercises the loop rather than a single decision.
 178:     Set-Reading @{ five_hour = (W 93 45) }
 179:     $job = Start-Job -ScriptBlock {
 180:         param($w, $s)
 181:         $o = & powershell -NoProfile -ExecutionPolicy Bypass -File $w -SnapshotPath $s -PollSeconds 2
 182:         [pscustomobject]@{ code = $LASTEXITCODE; out = ($o -join "`n") }
 183:     } -ArgumentList $wait, $snap
 184:     Start-Sleep -Seconds 3
 185:     Check 'parked, and still waiting after 3s' ($job.State -eq 'Running') 'True'
 186:     Set-Reading @{ five_hour = (W 8 300) }          # another session refreshed it; window reopened
 187:     $done = Wait-Job $job -Timeout 25
 188:     $r = Receive-Job $job; Remove-Job $job -Force
 189:     Check 'released once the reading cleared' ([bool]$done) 'True'
 190:     Check 'exit 0 on early release' $r.code 0
 191:     Check 'said it cleared early' ([bool]($r.out -match 'cleared early')) 'True'
 192: 
 193:     # A park must survive its own instrument breaking. The snapshot disappearing mid-wait leaves
 194:     # the target it captured at the start, so it still releases on the clock rather than hanging.
 195:     Set-Reading @{ five_hour = (W 94 0.6) }
 196:     $job2 = Start-Job -ScriptBlock {
 197:         param($w, $s)
 198:         $o = & powershell -NoProfile -ExecutionPolicy Bypass -File $w -SnapshotPath $s -PollSeconds 2
 199:         [pscustomobject]@{ code = $LASTEXITCODE; out = ($o -join "`n") }
 200:     } -ArgumentList $wait, $snap
 201:     Start-Sleep -Seconds 2
 202:     Remove-Item $snap -Force                        # the sensor dies mid-park
 203:     $done2 = Wait-Job $job2 -Timeout 180
 204:     $r2 = Receive-Job $job2; Remove-Job $job2 -Force
 205:     Check 'survives its snapshot vanishing mid-park' ([bool]$done2) 'True'
 206:     Check 'and still releases on the clock' $r2.code 0
 207: 
 208:     ''
 209:     '8. Coming out the other side of a park'
 210:     # Everything above started with the reset already behind us. This is the real shape: park
 211:     # while the reset is still ahead, sit through it, and come out.
 212:     Set-Reading @{ five_hour = (W 96 0.15) }        # resets in ~9 seconds
 213:     $t0 = Get-Date
 214:     $out = & powershell -NoProfile -ExecutionPolicy Bypass -File $wait -SnapshotPath $snap -PollSeconds 2 -SlackMinutes 0
 215:     $elapsed = ((Get-Date) - $t0).TotalSeconds
 216:     Check 'crossed the reset boundary and released' $LASTEXITCODE 0
 217:     Check 'and actually waited for it, rather than short-circuiting' ([bool]($elapsed -ge 6)) 'True'
 218:     Check 'and said the stated reset had passed' ([bool]($out -match 'stated reset time has passed')) 'True'
 219: 
 220:     # The slack exists so a release cannot arrive before the provider has actually turned the
 221:     # window over. Reset half a minute gone, a minute of slack: still parked.
 222:     Set-Reading @{ five_hour = (W 96 -0.5) }
 223:     $job3 = Start-Job -ScriptBlock {
 224:         param($w, $s) & powershell -NoProfile -ExecutionPolicy Bypass -File $w -SnapshotPath $s -PollSeconds 2 | Out-Null
 225:     } -ArgumentList $wait, $snap
 226:     Start-Sleep -Seconds 4
 227:     Check 'still parked inside the slack, not released on the exact second' ($job3.State -eq 'Running') 'True'
 228:     Stop-Job $job3; Remove-Job $job3 -Force
 229: 
 230:     # WHAT THE COORDINATOR SEES NEXT. Its first turn after the park renders the status line, so
 231:     # the reading is fresh. Three outcomes, and all three must be distinguishable.
 232:     Set-Reading @{ five_hour = (W 2 300) }
 233:     Check 'a genuinely new window -> release the work' (Verdict) 'OK'
 234:     # The reset can roll forward: the window turned over but the level did not fall enough, or a
 235:     # different window is now the blocker. Re-park, do not proceed because the clock said so.
 236:     Set-Reading @{ five_hour = (W 94 300) }
 237:     Check 'still over the line after the reset -> park again' (Verdict) 'PAUSE'
 238:     & powershell -NoProfile -ExecutionPolicy Bypass -File $wait -SnapshotPath $snap -PollSeconds 1 -MaxHours 0 | Out-Null
 239:     Check 'and the park is re-enterable' $LASTEXITCODE 1
 240:     # The degradation path: nothing refreshed the reading, so it is old AND its reset has gone by.
 241:     # The old number now proves nothing, so this is UNKNOWN - reported, not halted, by the rule.
 242:     Set-Reading @{ five_hour = (W 96 -20) } 45
 243:     Check 'nothing refreshed it after the park -> UNKNOWN, not a false PAUSE' (Verdict) 'UNKNOWN'
 244:     # And the weekly window is unmoved by a five-hour reset - a release must not be granted by the
 245:     # wrong window turning over.
 246:     Set-Reading @{ five_hour = (W 1 300); seven_day = (W 93 5000) }
 247:     Check 'the five-hour reset does not release a weekly block' (Verdict) 'PAUSE'
 248:     Check 'and the weekly one is still named as the blocker' (Gauge).worstWindow 'seven_day'
 249: 
 250:     ''
 251:     "RESULT: {0} passed, {1} failed" -f $pass, $fail
 252:     if ($fail -gt 0) { ''; 'failed:'; $failed | ForEach-Object { '  - ' + $_ }; exit 1 }
 253: } finally {
 254:     Remove-Item $snap -Force -ErrorAction SilentlyContinue
 255:     Get-Job | Where-Object { $_.Command -match 'window-wait' } | Remove-Job -Force -ErrorAction SilentlyContinue
 256: }

===== loop/work/window-alarm.cmd =====
   1: @echo off
   2: REM window-alarm.cmd - PostToolUse / PostToolUseFailure: put the window alarm in front of the model.
   3: REM
   4: REM WHY A BATCH FILE AND NOT POWERSHELL. This runs after EVERY tool call in the system, in every
   5: REM agent, forever. A `powershell -NoProfile` process costs roughly 200-400 ms on this machine and
   6: REM would be paid on every one of them. `findstr` is an order of magnitude cheaper. The cost is
   7: REM the whole reason the sensor composes the line and this only reads it.
   8: REM
   9: REM THE CHECKPOINT READS, IT NEVER COMPUTES. statusline.ps1 writes exactly one line to
  10: REM window-verdict.txt on every refresh - OK, or ALARM ... , or UNKNOWN ... - and the first token
  11: REM is the anchor. All this does is match that anchor and hand the line back.
  12: REM
  13: REM Exit 2 with text on stderr is the documented channel that shows the text to the model. Exit 0
  14: REM is silence. There is no third answer: UNKNOWN is deliberately silent here, because a running
  15: REM subagent can neither act on a broken sensor nor fix it, and an UNKNOWN alarm would fire on
  16: REM every tool call in the system for as long as the founder session sits idle. The actors who
